#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171780 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "dd9a6e6a-e3d1-5684-8a14-7da410461aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8642" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{rad/s}$. Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0$, find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O$, and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0$.\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0)$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7$.\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and\n\n$$\n\\omega= -3 \\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\n(negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 7(\\cos (-3t)i + \\sin (-3t)j) \\\\\n&= 7(\\cos 3ti - \\sin 3tj)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171781 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "cfcca273-3b96-5812-8b0d-029875f0585a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8643" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (5\\cos πti + 5\\sin πtj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-5π\\sin πti + 5π\\cos πtj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid v(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-5π\\sin πt)^{2} + (5π\\cos πt)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}\\sin^{2} πt + 25π^{2}\\cos^{2} πt} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}(\\sin^{2} πt + \\cos^{2} πt)} \\\\\n&= 5π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{m/s}$.\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = R \\mid \\omega \\mid\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nR=5\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = 5\\cdot \\mid \\pi \\mid = 5\\pi \\textrm{m/s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171782 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "5614ccb1-e2be-5944-8229-61d62e5860fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8644" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\, \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (4(\\cos 3ti + \\sin 3tj))^{′} \\\\\n&=-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= (v(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-36\\cos 3ti - 36\\sin 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid a(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-36\\cos 3t)^{2} + (-36\\sin 3t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}\\cos^{2} 3t + 36^{2}\\sin^{2} 3t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}} \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nNote that $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad \\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = \\omega^2 \\mid \\mid \\mathbf r \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\na=4\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = 3^2\\cdot \\mid 4 \\mid = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd0486bc-01ac-57ae-b0b7-5eec452ce235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171428} {:answer/id #uuid "177138e7-36ea-576a-9be0-ae3fe52ab6cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171429} {:answer/id #uuid "27017d8c-e390-5ee9-913c-ea73beeaf09a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171430} {:answer/id #uuid "398eee85-b5e6-55a7-bc3d-7c18044cf9a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171431} {:answer/id #uuid "4a11faeb-02ce-56c8-b5bd-fe1d3c445fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd0486bc-01ac-57ae-b0b7-5eec452ce235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171428} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8583124-a731-5a9c-96e1-bb0dcc3c10ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171427}] :question/id #uuid "ac5215be-1a4b-5b45-94e1-594b34acf8a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97541" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t) = 2\\cos 3ti + 2\\sin 3tj$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e74c10-6010-5050-b7c0-c210e0b4ea3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i - 6j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171446} {:answer/id #uuid "8824dff0-6218-523e-89ff-b9077023524b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\sqrt{3}i - 12j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171447} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9b3d2f-dc72-58e6-8e97-ac067586b54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\sqrt{3}i + \\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171448} {:answer/id #uuid "3b650509-030d-5225-9e1c-80dea26a5af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171449} {:answer/id #uuid "42b5db76-f8bd-593a-bb98-2597e94188ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i - 6\\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42b5db76-f8bd-593a-bb98-2597e94188ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6i - 6\\sqrt{3}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171450} :answer-field/id #uuid "496df0ec-9beb-508c-969d-793cb6918805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171445}] :question/id #uuid "3b6b507b-dfd6-5936-a7e2-413872437421" :question/math-academy-id "q-97568" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2rad/s$. Given that the particle is located at $(3, 0)$ when $t = 0$, find the acceleration of the particle at $t = \\frac{π}{6}$ seconds."}]}
